WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.666
Your payments API logs one line per request.

2
00:00:05.666 --> 00:00:13.956
To count requests per path, the quickest way is a hash map in memory: read each line once, and add one to its path's count.

3
00:00:14.133 --> 00:00:20.742
But the map needs an entry for every different path, and every payment has its own page.

4
00:00:20.742 --> 00:00:25.773
Yesterday's log has about 350,000 of them, at about 111 bytes each.

5
00:00:25.933 --> 00:00:30.372
The map can't drop an entry until the last line is read.

6
00:00:30.372 --> 00:00:37.744
In the lesson's demo, a 64 megabyte heap ran out after about 617,000 paths, and the job died.

7
00:00:37.933 --> 00:00:45.011
Sorting brings equal keys together too, and an external merge sort never needs all the keys in memory at once.

8
00:00:45.011 --> 00:00:48.421
Shrink it to see how: twelve keys, and room for four.

9
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.525
Read four keys, sort them in memory, and write them to disk.

10
00:00:52.525 --> 00:00:55.141
A sorted file like this is called a run.

11
00:00:55.333 --> 00:00:56.568
Do that twice more.

12
00:00:56.568 --> 00:01:01.832
The input has been read once, and every key now sits in one of three sorted runs.

13
00:01:02.033 --> 00:01:04.459
Now merge all three runs in one pass.

14
00:01:04.459 --> 00:01:07.999
Memory holds just the first key of each run, its head.

15
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.981
Take the smallest head and write it out.

16
00:01:10.981 --> 00:01:14.081
Then refill from the run it came from, and repeat.

17
00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:19.820
When the runs are empty, the output is sorted, and memory never held more than three keys.

18
00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:26.035
Equal keys now sit side by side, so a single scan with one counter finds every count.

19
00:01:26.233 --> 00:01:32.200
At full size, 100 gigabytes of keys and a 1 gigabyte buffer make 100 runs.

20
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:38.005
The sort command merges 16 at a time by default, so it needs two passes.

21
00:01:38.005 --> 00:01:40.504
At 128 at a time, it needs one.

22
00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:44.613
A hash map is fastest while the different keys fit in memory.

23
00:01:44.613 --> 00:01:46.666
A sort never runs out of memory.

24
00:01:46.666 --> 00:01:49.168
It just gets slower as the input grows.
